WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.940
Glucose enters and hexokinase traps it as glucose 6-phosphate, the true branch point

2
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.220
of central metabolism.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.480
From here, the molecule faces a real choice.

4
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:19.300
Phosphoglucose isomerase channels it leftward into glycolysis, where the payoff is

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:24.060
ATP and pyruvate, with ATP the energy currency the cell spends.

6
00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.440
Alternatively, glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase commits it rightward into the

7
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.940
pentose phosphate pathway, whose products are NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and

8
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.420
ribose 5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis.

9
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.740
The deciding factor is simple.

10
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.680
What does the cell need right now?

11
00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.480
Energy or building blocks?

12
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.700
This fork captures the entire logic of why the pentose phosphate pathway exists

13
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.180
alongside glycolysis.

14
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:57.563
NAD and NADP look almost identical, differing by a single phosphate, yet the cell

15
00:00:57.563 --> 00:01:01.323
keeps them in dramatically different states for dramatically different purposes.

16
00:01:02.243 --> 00:01:08.263
The NAD pool, free in the cytosol, sits oxidized, roughly a thousand to one in favor

17
00:01:08.263 --> 00:01:13.743
of NAD⁺, +, because catabolism needs an electron acceptor ready to grab electrons

18
00:01:13.743 --> 00:01:20.583
from fuel. The NADP pool is the mirror image, held strongly toward NADPH, because

19
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:24.743
anabolism and antioxidant defense need an electron donor on tap.

20
00:01:25.663 --> 00:01:28.003
Same backbone, opposite redox poise.

21
00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:33.483
By maintaining these as separate cytosolic pools, the cell can run breakdown and

22
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:38.583
biosynthesis simultaneously without the two electron economies interfering with each

23
00:01:38.583 --> 00:01:39.583
other.

24
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:44.533
Reactive oxygen species are oxygen-derived molecules, many with an unpaired

25
00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:48.373
electron, that attack and damage proteins, lipids, and DNA.

26
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:53.213
The cell's frontline defense is glutathione, which neutralizes these threats by

27
00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:56.073
taking the oxidative hit so other molecules don't have to.

28
00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:01.633
But once glutathione absorbs that hit, it becomes oxidized and spent, linked into

29
00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:07.253
the GSSG dimer. To get it back into the reduced protective GSH form, the cell spends

30
00:02:07.253 --> 00:02:11.153
NADPH. And where does that NADPH come from?

31
00:02:11.653 --> 00:02:14.273
The pentose phosphate pathway, a major source.

32
00:02:14.993 --> 00:02:17.913
So G6PD supports antioxidant defense.

33
00:02:18.593 --> 00:02:24.033
Lose that enzyme, and cells struggle to regenerate glutathione, so oxidative damage

34
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:26.873
can run unchecked, red blood cells most of all.

35
00:02:27.817 --> 00:02:32.277
The non-oxidative phase is best understood as carbon bookkeeping carried out by two

36
00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:35.077
enzymes, transketolase and transaldolase.

37
00:02:35.457 --> 00:02:36.457
Track the carbons.

38
00:02:36.937 --> 00:02:41.497
First, transketolase takes two five-carbon sugars and produces a three-carbon and a

39
00:02:41.497 --> 00:02:42.517
seven-carbon sugar.

40
00:02:43.177 --> 00:02:47.157
Then transaldolase converts that three plus seven into a six and a four-carbon

41
00:02:47.157 --> 00:02:52.877
sugar. Finally, transketolase acts again, turning a four and a five into a six and a

42
00:02:52.877 --> 00:02:55.677
three. Sum it all and the net result is clean.

43
00:02:55.677 --> 00:03:02.097
Three five-carbon sugars become two six-carbon sugars and one three-carbon sugar.

44
00:03:02.757 --> 00:03:07.737
That is how excess pentoses are recycled back into glycolytic intermediates when the

45
00:03:07.737 --> 00:03:09.537
cell needs energy more than ribose.

46
00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:16.037
The pentose phosphate pathway is not one fixed route, but four operating modes, and

47
00:03:16.037 --> 00:03:18.237
the cell selects among them based on what it needs.

48
00:03:18.897 --> 00:03:23.257
When it needs ribose for nucleotides but little NADPH, it runs only the

49
00:03:23.257 --> 00:03:24.297
non-oxidative phase.

50
00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:30.137
When it needs both NADPH and ribose in balance, it runs the oxidative phase straight

51
00:03:30.137 --> 00:03:35.857
through. When it needs lots of NADPH but little ribose, it routes ribose back into

52
00:03:35.857 --> 00:03:38.137
glycolytic intermediates and recycles them.

53
00:03:38.777 --> 00:03:44.317
And when it needs NADPH plus ATP, it pushes carbon all the way into pyruvate.

54
00:03:44.977 --> 00:03:49.337
One pathway, four behaviors, each a different combination of glycolysis,

55
00:03:49.797 --> 00:03:51.337
gluconeogenesis, and the shunt.
